From 0ee88beb2d96d068de7f0f7358088ec0c63a58de Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 16:57:01 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Ambient life under the new look: fireflies, falling leaves,
 motes in the sun and butterflies

Elwynn's woods feel lived in now, as WoW Forever's do. A few small things
move near the camera, never many and never far from it.

Fireflies come out after dusk (19:45 to 21:00) and go before first light:
warm green-gold glows low over the grass, more under the trees and over
the water, each blinking softly on its own beat and drifting slowly.
They are drawn bright enough for the bloom, and the three nearest that
are flashing light the ground a little through the look's light table.

By day leaves fall from the crowns of the trees near the camera (the
doodads whose model is a tree, by the same model-path test the wind
sways them by): a few at a time, green with some gold and rust, tumbling
and swinging as they fall and carried downwind in the look's wind, more
as a gust front passes the tree (the shaders' gust, now on the CPU too).
They land and fade.

Motes of pollen and dust drift in the air, lit only where the sun reaches
them (a ray toward the sun through the world's collision, and the crowns
between with gaps in their leaves, so the light comes in shafts), seen
mostly looking toward the sun, and as bright as the grade's god rays at
that hour: they sparkle at dawn and golden hour and are gone in shade.

A couple of butterflies flutter over open ground by day, two flapping
wings on a wandering flight, from a sprite drawn here (1.12 has none).

Rain stops the fireflies, the butterflies and the motes, softly over a
few seconds; the leaves keep falling, more and faster; while the ground
is still wet after rain, more motes hang in the sun.

Everything spawns within 20 to 40 yd of the camera in pools allocated
once, fades in where it is born and out at the edge of its radius, and
is drawn on the effect stream from one small atlas made at startup: two
draws a frame. Nothing is drawn on the ground but a landed leaf for a
moment. Indoors and under water there is none.

worldLife 0 turns it off on its own; looklife <0-3> scales how much
there is for film and looklife grade follows the hour again. worldLook 0
draws none of it.

Revised after the first films in the real client (#272, take 273):

- Fireflies barely showed: pinpoint pale specks, each lit about 11% of the
  time. A flash is long and soft now (width 0.35 of the period, was 0.11)
  over a glow that never goes out (0.2, was 0.06), the glow is bigger
  (0.45 yd, was 0.32) and warmer and stronger (2.1, 2.3, 0.55), enough for
  the bloom.
- Almost no leaves fell at Crystal Lake (1 out at the grade's density). A
  tree no longer has to be named a tree or a canopy: any swaying plant that
  is not a bush, a fern or the like, at least 3 yd tall, and its crown is
  the box of its leaves (its alpha-tested batches' vertices, measured once
  a model), else of its whole model, where the header box drew it before.
  looklife says how many trees are near and how many crowns drop leaves.
- Motes were invisible even with 270 out. They are bigger (0.13 yd, was
  0.07) and brighter (gain 3.0, was 1.7, against 0081's golden rays now
  0.6), seen over a wider lobe toward the sun (3, was 6), and the crowns'
  shafts are more open (0.6 shut, was 0.78). looklife says how many the sun
  reaches.

Revised after the fourth films (#272, take 280):

- Only 2 to 6 fireflies showed of 27 to 60 out: born evenly all round the
  camera, three in four were behind or beside it, and the far quarter
  fades at the edge. Three in four fireflies and motes are now born
  within 50 degrees of where the camera looks (those stay put when it
  turns, so nothing pops), and the fireflies are warmer (2.5, 2.0, 0.45,
  was 2.1, 2.3, 0.55).
- Only 2 to 6 leaves fell from 24 crowns, even at looklife 3: a crown
  dropped 0.012 a second. It drops about six times as many now (0.08,
  plus 0.12 in the wind and 0.6 in a gust), about 20 falling in a still
  wood.
- The motes were still invisible: bigger (0.22 yd, was 0.13) and
  brighter (gain 5, was 3), and more of them show looking away from the
  sun (0.25 of the glow toward it, was 0.12).
- looklife answered with the counts from before the change ("held at 0"
  said 60 fireflies). It now says what the new setting asks for at this
  hour, then the counts out before the change, and how many fireflies
  are ahead of the camera.

Revised after the fifth films (#272, take 291):

- Leaves: 24 crowns at Crystal Lake kept only 3 to 7 out where their rates
  want 20 to 40. A crown whose roll came up and whose spot had no ground
  under it, or under a yard of air, wasted the roll; it now tries four
  spots. And looklife now says what became of the leaves since it last
  ran (the rolls, the leaves born, the spots refused and why, how each
  leaf ended and its mean life), so the next film shows which limit holds.
- The nearest motes showed as big glowing orbs. No mote is drawn more than
  14 px across now, so one at arm's length is a speck.
- Fireflies were pale yellow. Their glow and their light are amber now.

Seventh round (take 310):
- Leaves lived 2 to 3 s: born in their crowns' lower half, a yard or so
  up, they were down in a second or two. A leaf is now born about its
  crown's middle and at least 2.5 yd up (six tries, was four), falls at
  0.45 to 0.8 yd/s (was 0.7 to 1.2) and lies 6 s (was 2.5).

